  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 201 AM EST Sun Nov 8 2026\n\nWeak mid level ridging remains in place across the region heading \ninto Tuesday while surface high pressure to the north gradually \nshifts eastward. Heading into the middle of the week, the mid level \nridging will gradually start to give way to a mid level trough as a \nshortwave impulse passes off to the north of the region. At the \nsurface, high pressure will gradually weaken over the area on \nWednesday as it pulls away to the northeast. While most of Tuesday \nwill remain on the drier side, some moisture advection will take\nplace on Wednesday as the mid level wind flow becomes more\nsouthwesterly. This additional moisture moving into the region \nmay be enough to introduce a low end chance of showers during the \nafternoon when instability will be maximized due to peak diurnal \nheating. High temperatures both on Tuesday and Wednesday will \ngenerally rise into the lower to mid 80s across most areas. Some \nlocations south of Alligator Alley in Southwest Florida may see \nhighs in the upper 80s each day. \n\nMoving into the second portion of the week, the weather pattern \nacross the region will change as mid level troughing will rapidly \namplify over the Eastern Seaboard during this time frame. At the \nsurface, a developing area of low pressure will push eastward from \nthe Midwest into the Mid-Atlantic states and then eventually off the \nNortheastern coastline during this time frame. The frontal boundary \nassociated with this system will gradually approach and move through \nSouth Florida during the end of the week timeframe. Uncertainty \nstill remains rather high in this part of the forecast as the \nglobal and ensemble guidance suite is still in disagreement in \nregards to the timing of this frontal passage across the region. \nThe ECMWF suite continues to show a more progressive and faster \nfrontal passage while the GFS solutions do show a slower passage \nthrough the area. While the exact details still need to be worked \nout over the next several days, it does look like chances of \nshowers will increase heading into the Thursday and Friday \ntimeframe across South Florida as the front pushes through. While \nthunderstorm chances look to be on the lower end at this point due\nto the best dynamics and instability remaining off to the north \nof the region, this will need to continue to be monitored to make\nsure these trends continue. \n\nBehind the front, strong high pressure will build back into the \nregion from the northwest causing winds to shift and increase \nrapidly out of the west northwest as cold air advection takes\nover. While uncertainty may remain high in regards to the timing\nof the frontal passage during the end of the week, trends are \npointing towards below normal temperatures and much drier \nconditions heading into next weekend. This will continue to be \nmonitored as the week progresses.",
